'' WHERE THE START POSITION SITS IS NOT THE SAME FOR THE TWO OF THEM:
''
''   instr( start, text, pattern )      first, and passed first
''   instrrev( text, pattern, start )   THIRD, and passed last
''
'' Taking the first argument for INSTRREV read the TEXT as a start and the INDEX as a
'' pattern, so the second descriptor could not be built and the call was DROPPED --
'' and the statement counted itself as lowered, because its arguments had been.
''
'' 'instrrev( s, any p )' is its own entry point, fb_StrInstrRevAny, and was declined
'' for want of one. The WIDE half had no lowering at all, and which family a call
'' belongs to cannot always be read off the first operand: 'instr( ut, up )' over a UDT
'' with a cast to wstring has no wide type anywhere in the expression, so the cast
'' overload is what decides.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --dump-ir tests/mir/src/instr-family.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: verify: ok
'' EXPECT-OUT: 0 statements not lowered
'' GOLDEN: expected/instr-family.out
